WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.670
- So happy Thursday everybody.
Welcome to the Data Science

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:16.710
Hangout. I hope
everyone's having a great week.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.400
If we haven't met before,
I'm Rachael Dempsey.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.120
I lead our community at Posit.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.790
If this is your first time ever
joining us at a Data

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:27.830
Science
Hangout, welcome.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:31.800
This is our open space
to chat about data science leadership,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.750
questions you're facing,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.960
and getting to hear about
what's going on in the world of

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.840
data across many
different industries and companies.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.890
And so we're here
every Thursday,

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:44.570
except for July,

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.590
at the same time, same place.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.960
So if you're watching
this recording on YouTube later,

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.240
you can also add these events to
your own calendar using the link below.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:54.650
But together,

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.590
we're all dedicated to
making this a welcoming environment

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.560
for everyone. So we love
to hear from everybody,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.170
no matter your level of experience,
the area of work or industry.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:11.480
It is also totally OK to just listen
in and just hang out here with us.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.930
There's also three ways you
can jump in and either ask

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:19.040
questions or provide your own
perspective on certain topics, too. So

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.320
you can always jump in
by raising your hand on Zoom,

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.090
and I'll keep an eye out.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.820
You can put questions
in the Zoom chat.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.790
And feel free to just put a
little star next to it if you

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.500
want me to read it
out loud instead,

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.220
if you're maybe in a
busy coffee shop or something.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.070
And then, third--

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.670
we also have a Slido link
where you can ask questions

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:38.720
anonymously.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.390
And I think Hannah or Curtis
will share that in the chat

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.330
here in just a second.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:49.120
But I am so excited to
have my colleague Joe Cheng joining us

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.460
here today as our
featured leader.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:56.020
Joe is the CTO and first
employee at Posit--

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.660
well, back then, RStudio

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:03.070
where he helped create the
RStudio IDE and Shiny web framework,

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:07.440
along with countless
complementary tools and packages.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:08.009
And Joe,

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:11.130
I'd love to get things kicked
off here by having you also

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:15.150
introduce yourself and just
share a little bit about

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.230
your role and what it
means to be CTO at Posit.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.520
- Yeah,

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.410
thanks for having me.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.430
CTO

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.400
is actually an honorary title.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.440
There are a lot of different
kinds of CTOs in the industry.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:30.840
And

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.030
one of them is

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.970
basically an engineer
that joined very early,

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.070
and it's an honorary title.

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:39.180
And that's what it--

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.180
certainly it started
out as for me. It

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.320
started out as purely
an acknowledgment that

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.620
there are
strategic conversations that

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.730
the board and the executive team
would like me to be part of.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.500
But my day to day was still
100% software engineer and then team lead.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.580
And these days,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.080
I think probably 90-plus
percent of my time is as a team

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.450
lead on the Shiny team.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.780
It's a team of about 11. And

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.040
I,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.680
every once in a while, will

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.210
do more CTO stuff,

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:18.930
which is having conversations that
are more at the strategic

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:22.560
level with the other execs
to talk about the direction that

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.130
we're taking the company in.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:30.960
So for my day to
day, I try to do

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.660
50% coding and 50%

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:38.850
leading the team. That usually is
more like 15% coding and 85%

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.260
leading the team.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.410
There's always emergencies and

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.280
last minute conversations
that come up.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.400
So I do a lot of code
reviews. I do a ton of

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.270
helping to decide feature
directions. I do some talking

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.300
with customers. I
do some support.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.760
Just really anything
that comes up.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.850
And we try to run our team
that way, for the most part.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:05.160
Most of our

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.680
roles are relatively
loose and flexible.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.360
So we really value having
everyone on the team,

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.589
or certainly all the software
engineers to be really thoughtful about

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:19.019
what kind of features would be
interesting to add to Shiny to

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:22.350
be able to design their
own features to implement and

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:26.220
to be responsible for the quality
and support of their own features.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.790
So yes, for me, it's
Shiny all the time--

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.740
Shiny day and nights,

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:37.110
Shiny for R, Shiny for
Python, packages that compliment Shiny,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.540
HTML widgets.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.740
For the last month,
I've been working on a--

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.350
sort of the equivalent
to the DT for R,

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:49.290
which is a way to
view data tables--

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:52.380
data frames and data tables
very quickly with a rich HTML

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.980
interface. I've been working
on that for Shiny for Python,

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.220
where we have not had that
capability in the past.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:02.010
So that's one particular
feature that I've been working

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.750
on. But yeah, for sure,

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:08.130
most of my time is in
meetings and helping other team members

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.870
achieve what they need to.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:15.370
- Thanks, Joe, and I forgot to
ask you this, but I usually ask everybody--

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:19.300
what's something that you like
to do outside of work, too?

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.190
- For me, that's cycling.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:24.940
I don't do it nearly
as much as I'd like to,

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:29.170
but that's my favorite way to blow
off some steam after a long day.

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:33.890
- Perfect. Thank you.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.250
So while we're waiting
for some questions

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:38.450
to come in from everybody here,

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:41.590
thank you so much for
the intro. And I'm wondering--

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.870
if somebody had never
heard about Shiny before,

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:47.510
how would you describe it?

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:50.090
- Yeah, so

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:50.630
to me,

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:56.750
Shiny is fundamentally about
letting data scientists create interactive

00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:00.110
web applications to

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:03.200
take their analysis
that they're

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.840
already doing on their own
machine and taking the insights

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.360
that they are getting from
their data on their own machine

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:11.150
and being able to
expose that to--

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:13.070
usually other people,
sometimes to themselves,

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:16.100
but usually to expose that
to other people in a really

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:19.610
dynamic and interactive way.

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:20.900
In

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:24.260
the past, before Shiny, I think

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.000
you would see--

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:26.360
I mean,

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:29.660
it's still very common to issue
a static report and send it to

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:33.110
someone. And if there were
lots of variables in your analysis--

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:35.330
if there were lots of different
ways you might want to slice

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:36.350
and dice the data,

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:43.070
you would either just add more and
more pages to that PDF or HTML page.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:46.280
And we had a lot of people tell
us that they would exchange

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.410
PDFs with thousands of pages,

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.540
and good luck trying to
find the page that you want.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:53.180
Or you would do this
back and forth of, OK,

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:54.830
here's my best guess
at what you want,

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:56.930
and then you'd have this
endless email back and forth

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:58.850
of, how about this? How
about this? How about this,

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:01.590
until they find the needle in
the Haystack that they're looking for.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:02.810
So

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:05.300
that's just one example of

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:08.030
when it might be useful to take

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:11.120
insights that you can easily
gain for yourself on your own

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:12.860
console, in your own notebook,

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:16.550
and to be able to
turn that into something that empowers

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.440
other people in your
organization or that you're

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:19.520
collaborating with,

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.550
or just that you think
might find your data interesting,

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:27.950
to be able to interact with that
data in a more dynamic and modern way.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:32.540
That's how it started. That
and teaching statistics--

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.280
that was the other big

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:39.560
thing that we were very focused
on in the beginning. And

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:43.130
boy, it's been interesting
what people have taken

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:44.790
Shiny and used it for.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:48.410
So in terms of what it's for--

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:50.210
everything from

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:52.160
BI to

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:56.600
machine learning and large
language models and games and

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:59.780
fantasy football
draft analyzers,

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:03.660
all sorts of interesting things
people have done with it.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.890
But one of the most
important things for us

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:11.090
is that it is accessible
for anyone who calls themselves a

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:13.190
data scientist. So
you do need to code.

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:14.640
That is very important to us.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.220
That's one of the core
tenets of our company,

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:19.100
is that we really
believe in code first.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:23.220
But you don't have to be
able to code in web development technologies.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:26.540
You don't need to be a web
developer to create Shiny apps.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:28.550
You just need to be

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:30.550
conversant in the
language of data science.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:34.570
- Thank you.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:38.840
And I see an anonymous question
that came in a little bit ago,

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.559
which was, what's
the elevator pitch,

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:43.750
and then maybe further
discussion points,

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:45.400
for investing in Shiny,

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:48.550
rather than using
point-and-click BI tools?

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:51.190
- Yeah, absolutely,
that's a good question.

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:53.500
And it's interesting--

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.720
I had one thing in mind
when I created Shiny,

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:58.330
and then that has evolved
as I've gotten feedback from

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:04.300
people who have spent a lot of
time using both BI tools and Shiny.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:08.350
So my elevator pitch in the
beginning was that BI tools are

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:12.520
great, and you should use Shiny
when those run out of steam.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:14.770
In the beginning, that
was really about advanced analytics.

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:19.090
So if you want to just
draw some scatter plots and line charts--

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:21.610
I mean, BI tools can do a lot.
I'm not trying to be glib.

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:24.700
But if what you're trying to
do is completely within the

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:27.280
wheelhouse of BI tools, great.

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:31.090
No problem. Those
are awesome tools.

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:32.470
And

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:34.780
if you find yourself

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:38.590
wanting to use a predictive
model that they don't support--

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:41.380
and there are a lot that
they don't support--

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:44.230
or you want to pull from
some data source that is more

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:50.500
dynamic than a BI tool is
designed for, then use shiny. So it's--

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:53.410
for the simple stuff,
sure, use a BI tool--

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:57.500
I mean, if you can afford
it. A lot of them aren't cheap.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.410
But then, for the
other stuff, use Shiny.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:04.150
And I actually had a number
of people tell me that

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:06.280
it was actually not
like that for them--

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:08.800
that, in fact,

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:11.680
numerous times I've heard people
have to deploy to BI

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.000
tools within
their organization, or,

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:18.730
if they're consultants
and they're at a client,

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:24.340
that the only deployment target they
can feasibly use is, say, Tableau,

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:28.330
they would actually build it in
Shiny first and then import it

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:29.780
to Tableau. And I was like,

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:31.660
you're going to have to explain
to me why you're doing that.

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:33.430
That is very surprising.

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:34.510
And

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:37.450
what they said to me was that

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:43.120
a number of the advantages of
just using code in the first place

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.400
were just such big advantages
that they didn't want to give

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:48.010
them up when they
were using Tableau.

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:51.010
So first of all, it was
very common, they would say,

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:54.550
for them to work really hard
and come up with this very

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:58.870
sophisticated Tableau
application and then--

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:00.160
or Tableau dashboard.

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:03.520
And then the stakeholder would
say, oh, that looks great.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:07.990
Please just change this or that
thing and this or that thing.

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:10.840
Because of the way these
point-and-click tools work,

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:12.820
you would have to start
the whole thing over. I mean,

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:14.590
you would have all the insights
you gained along the way,

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:16.120
but the mechanics
of building it--

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:18.880
you would have to build
it again from scratch, whereas

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:24.010
for a Shiny app, you could
just replace a single line to

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:25.360
use a different data source.

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:26.770
Or

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:28.690
sometimes they would
be like, that's great,

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:33.910
and we want the same dashboard
now for these 10 other departments--

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:35.410
these 10 other
scenarios. And again,

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:37.270
you'd be in this position of--

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:40.780
the reuse was really a
lot harder than when you were

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:42.370
dealing with code.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:43.390
So that was one.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.580
The second people

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:48.580
told me was that the
extensibility was really

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:51.100
frustrating for them.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:53.684
To build

00:11:53.684 --> 00:11:56.540
a BI dashboard that serves
you well for a while,

00:11:56.540 --> 00:12:01.100
but then to know that there
is always the possibility of some

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:04.640
new requirement arriving that
they would not be able to

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:09.080
satisfy, whereas Shiny having
a ceiling that's essentially--

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:11.150
by comparison, it's infinite--

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:13.520
was very comforting to them.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:15.110
And the third is that--

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:16.730
the

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:18.860
feedback I received was that,
if you know how to code,

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:23.330
that doing a Shiny dashboard
in code is just more fun than

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:25.280
the point and
click, especially--

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:28.340
when you know what you want to do
and you know how you would code it,

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:30.860
it's frustrating to try
to figure out how the

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:34.460
point-and-click interface
wants you to express that.

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:36.590
And then, finally,

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:41.510
just being pragmatic, people
told me that it's

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:44.780
a better resume builder to have
built a bunch of Shiny apps

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:46.940
than to have built
a bunch of Tableau

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:47.630
point-and-click dashboards,

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:50.840
that had led them to better
career opportunities.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:53.390
I'm just being
real with you guys.

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:55.490
That's not something like
we put on the website,

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:58.700
but I've heard
that too many times

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:03.240
to think that that's not a real
motivation for some people.

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:06.254
Shiny, because we're all
control freaks. Totally, Luke.

00:13:06.254 --> 00:13:07.230
- [LAUGHS]

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:07.470
Joe,

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:10.980
a few people are commenting that
was a very long elevator ride.

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:16.912
- Yeah, I tend to do that.

00:13:16.912 --> 00:13:18.560
- [LAUGHS]

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:21.130
No, that was great
though, Thank you so much.

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:23.980
Ethan, I see you had asked
a question in the Zoom chat.

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:25.720
Do you want to jump in here?

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.610
- Yeah, sure, hi, Joe.

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.470
I actually haven't tried

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:30.520
Shiny Python yet,

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:33.400
but just want to get
you some high-level thoughts when you're

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:34.870
developing Shiny Python.

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:39.340
Is anything that you've learned
from developing it for offers?

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:41.440
And then

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:46.030
in terms of feature parity, is
it a asset or liability for the

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:48.355
engineering team when
developing Shiny for Python?

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:53.820
- Yeah, very good questions.

00:13:55.990 --> 00:14:01.180
I'd say one of the biggest
things we learned--

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:04.630
I'll say there's
two main things that

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:07.870
come to mind when thinking
about the process of developing

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:09.940
Shiny for Python.

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:10.930
One--

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:12.340
well, I'd say this isn't
something I learned,

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:14.515
but definitely some other
people on the team learned--

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:18.370
jumping to Python

00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:21.070
was not as scary as it sounded.

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:24.370
I think, for a lot of people
that were on my team, R--

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:27.250
and JavaScript but mostly R
was the language that they learned

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:30.760
to program in and have
invested many, many, many years

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:32.230
of their lives and careers in.

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:34.270
And

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:37.210
there was a lot of
assumption, in the beginning,

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:40.240
that we ware going to have
to hire a bunch of Python experts.

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:44.550
And

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:46.250
I don't want to be--

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:48.560
I don't want to
oversimplify it because

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:52.250
I think it is not true that
you can just pick up a book and

00:14:52.250 --> 00:14:55.010
learn Python really
well in just a few days.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:58.130
You really need to spend
time with the language

00:14:58.130 --> 00:14:59.690
to learn beyond
just the syntax,

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:02.120
learn the true Pythonic
way to do things,

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:03.080
and that takes some time.

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:06.140
But when you are working full
time on a project with a bunch

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:08.570
of team members that are
going through the same thing,

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:12.470
it actually did not
take very long for our

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:16.580
devs to be quite productive in
Python. So that was one thing,

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:18.530
is that on a technical--

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:20.420
on the technical side,

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:24.140
it was not really a very
difficult transition for the

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:27.860
team to become bilingual.

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:33.230
And I think the second
thing is that in Python--

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:35.240
this might not be that
interesting for data science.

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:38.120
But for software engineering,

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:44.090
Python, in recent versions,
has gotten better and better at

00:15:44.090 --> 00:15:46.040
static type checking,

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:50.510
so the ability to mark up
your variables and function

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:54.800
arguments with not only the name
of a parameter but the type

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:59.870
that you expect it to be,
and then having tools that will

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:02.870
analyze your code and make
sure that every function is being

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:05.000
called in an appropriate way.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.990
Those tools, I think,

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:09.620
are mostly seen these days as
a liability in data science that,

00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:12.510
they slow you down because you
have to write this extra code.

00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:14.450
But when you are building
something like Shiny,

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:16.670
it's a huge help. And

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:18.020
that's one of the
things that I think--

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:20.180
coming away from

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:22.100
shipping the first versions of
Shiny for Python, it's

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:22.430
ah,

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:26.150
it would have been really nice
if we could have these same tools

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:29.375
on the R side to
help us maintain Shiny for R.

00:16:31.985 --> 00:16:32.630
Sorry. Can you remind me?

00:16:32.630 --> 00:16:35.330
What was the second
part of your question?

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:37.460
- Yeah, it's about
feature parity.

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:41.120
Do you see that as an
asset or liability for your engineering team?

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:43.940
- Yeah. So

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:47.570
do you mean feature parity
between R and Python in general,

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:50.240
or trying to achieve feature
parity between Shiny for R and

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:51.380
Shiny for Python?

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:53.140
- Shiny in specific.

00:16:53.140 --> 00:16:56.690
- Oh, OK, yeah. So

00:16:56.690 --> 00:17:01.850
I think it is definitely
a double-edged sword.

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:05.119
It's super helpful to have
built all this once before

00:17:05.119 --> 00:17:07.040
because so many problems--

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:08.300
we

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:09.890
not only have a solution for,

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:12.890
but we thought about multiple
solutions before we decided on

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:14.720
one. And we can go

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:20.180
back and reanalyze very quickly
without having to invent

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:23.849
too much stuff
from whole cloth.

00:17:23.849 --> 00:17:25.190
But

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:26.480
the tough thing, I think,

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:29.840
is that sometimes we
are a little bit--

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:30.680
we try not to,

00:17:30.680 --> 00:17:32.900
but we feel a little bit
stuck with the decisions that we've

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:33.840
made in the past.

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:37.730
So the biggest one that we've
been discussing to death--

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:41.300
sorry, we've been discussing--

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:42.080
is--

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:46.670
Shiny modules is a feature
for Shiny for R where,

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:48.050
if you want to--

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:49.640
if your app is
getting too complex--

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:52.760
there's too much stuff going on
in one server function or one

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:54.690
UI, you can break
it up into modules.

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:56.645
And those modules
can be reused.

00:17:56.645 --> 00:17:58.460
They're name spaced.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:01.280
It really helps you reason
about your app as a collection

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:03.110
of smaller pieces.

00:18:03.110 --> 00:18:05.810
In Python, we have
that feature as well.

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:07.700
It's also called
Shiny Modules. Well,

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:13.430
Python modules are a thing
in Python, and the name, modules--

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:15.770
for us to move away
from that for Python,

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:19.190
it means now we have two.
There are Shiny modules for R,

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:21.800
and then there are
Python components

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:25.650
for Shiny for Python. And that
is super annoying for all of us.

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:27.800
So I think there are a lot
of little things like this,

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:30.680
where we feel a little bit
beholden to the past because it

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:32.330
would be awkward to
have different versions,

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:36.140
but at the same time,
doesn't necessarily always fit 100%.

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:39.530
It hasn't been that bad, but
the times when it comes up,

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:43.230
it certainly is frustrating.

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:45.230
- Thank you. It's
a good perspective.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:49.360
- Libby, I see you had
a question in the chat.

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:52.100
Do you want to jump in here?

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:53.120
- Sure,

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:55.490
can you hear me? My
mic's been acting up.

00:18:55.490 --> 00:18:56.150
- Sounds good.

00:18:56.150 --> 00:18:57.920
- You can, OK.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:18:59.830
My question is--

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:02.200
if you could go back in time

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:03.980
to 15 years ago,

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:07.360
20 years ago and tell
yourself what you're doing right now--

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:09.740
what your career looks,
what your life looks like,

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:13.045
what do you think would
surprise your younger self the most?

00:19:17.090 --> 00:19:18.230
- Everything, everything.

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:20.230
- [LAUGHS]

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:26.450
- I've been unbelievably
fortunate in not only my career

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:33.500
but in my family life and
my relationship with my extended family.

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.500
My life has--

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:42.860
yeah, it's been an incredible
run. And I think there were

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:46.100
a set of things that I
really wanted to accomplish by the end

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:48.940
of my career, and

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:50.240
I don't know--

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:54.860
I sort of find myself
in my mid-forties

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:57.320
needing to cast a
new vision because

00:19:57.320 --> 00:20:01.100
all the things that I wanted to
do I have just had the great

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:04.700
privilege and been so fortunate
as to have those things

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:06.060
happen to me in my career.

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:09.140
And I think, in particular,

00:20:09.140 --> 00:20:13.350
15 years ago in my
career, I felt like

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:16.170
I had stumbled into

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:18.210
this incredible industry--

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:19.770
just talking about

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:21.930
tech in general,

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:24.150
that, back then, had
a very different feel.

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:27.240
It felt like we were--

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:33.030
it was not quite as mainstream, and
I felt like I had stumbled into this--

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:35.670
I mean, so fun--

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:37.980
like, such a fun way
to make a living,

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:41.400
to be writing
programs all day with

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:44.820
smart and interesting people, and

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:47.940
to feel like I had

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:50.910
all my natural
talents aligned with

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:53.700
the skills and interests
you need to be in software.

00:20:53.700 --> 00:20:58.230
And the thing that scared
me 15 years ago was--

00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:03.150
what if all of my career
contributions, through luck or

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:06.870
happenstance, add up to not
very much? That was my fear,

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:09.930
was that I have the
education for this.

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:14.770
I have the natural talent
for this. I have the opportunity.

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:16.740
But there's so
much luck involved.

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:19.530
Will I get the opportunity
to actually do something that is

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:21.530
interesting and meaningful?

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:25.230
And

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:29.010
when I was working on RStudio,
I was already feeling like, oh,

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:32.640
wow, this could really be
it. And then Shiny felt

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:38.010
like, OK, this is overdoing it a
little bit. But I mean, it was--

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:39.450
like I never had--

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:41.490
I never pictured myself

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:43.490
coming up with a truly--

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:47.890
I was going to say,
truly original idea.

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:50.950
The idea is 99% building on the

00:21:50.950 --> 00:21:54.700
shoulders of reactive work
that was done in JavaScript.

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:58.090
But even like the insight to apply
it in this new way on the

00:21:58.090 --> 00:22:01.240
server side to data science--

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:05.320
I never aspired to have
an idea like that. I just

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:07.000
aspired for somebody
else to have an idea,

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:10.570
and I would be able to help,
and for it to be really

00:22:10.570 --> 00:22:12.670
impactful. So

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:16.420
in our little corner of
the world that is data science,

00:22:16.420 --> 00:22:19.910
Shiny has had a bigger impact than I
was ever hoping for out of my career.

00:22:19.910 --> 00:22:23.410
So I think that would be
the most comforting thing for me, 15

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:27.550
years ago, to find out it's not
all going to add up to nothing.

00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:31.240
There is going to be some
good that comes out of the stuff

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:33.130
that you do, that

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:39.000
these ideas are going to
impact people's work and impact

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:42.550
the design of other packages
that have been created,

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:46.780
both in R and in Python. So
I'm just super, super grateful

00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:49.510
for that because that was--

00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:51.230
like, it was really
something that I was very,

00:22:51.230 --> 00:22:55.390
very anxious about, that it
would all add up to nothing.

00:22:55.390 --> 00:22:56.350
Oh, this got a little bit real.

00:22:56.350 --> 00:22:58.840
- Thank you. I don't think
you have to worry anymore,

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:02.770
about that concern. You're good.

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:05.050
- Thank you, Joe.
And I saw it--

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:08.740
and thank you for the question
Libby. I see Lisa said,

00:23:08.740 --> 00:23:11.260
please answer this without making
us tear up again like

00:23:11.260 --> 00:23:12.770
you did at Conf.

00:23:12.770 --> 00:23:14.590
[LAUGHS]

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:16.810
And it was just reminding me--

00:23:16.810 --> 00:23:19.570
I love listening to
you present, Joe,

00:23:19.570 --> 00:23:23.530
and how you tell stories and
how you can be vulnerable and

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:28.870
put emotion into your presentations.
And I was just wondering--

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:30.100
how

00:23:30.100 --> 00:23:32.200
do you learn that?

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:36.880
How did you learn how to get
up on stage and make us feel all

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:40.110
those emotions that we all
did at the RStudio conference?

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:44.170
- It's

00:23:44.170 --> 00:23:46.660
funny. I can tell you exactly

00:23:46.660 --> 00:23:47.470
how it happened. I mean,

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:50.620
I'd given a lot of
presentations before that one,

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:54.090
but that was the first
one that I was like,

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:55.940
what if I made this

00:23:55.940 --> 00:23:59.210
90% about the
technology but 10%

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:00.800
share something that

00:24:00.800 --> 00:24:03.540
cost me a little bit?

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:05.550
And

00:24:05.550 --> 00:24:08.820
it was actually because I

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:11.310
attend a local church,

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:13.680
and

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:15.510
there was this
pastor who got up--

00:24:15.510 --> 00:24:16.830
he and his wife--

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:20.580
and they, in front of
all these people and being livestreamed,

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:26.910
talked about how he had cheated on
her in the first year of their marriage.

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:28.200
And I mean, it had been--

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:28.410
I don't know--

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.600
20, 30 years. But still,

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:33.360
that the two of them would
get up and talk about

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:34.590
the most painful thing,

00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:37.650
the most painful betrayal that
he ever inflicted on her--

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:39.630
that they would both get
up and talk about it--

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:41.630
I was like, holy crap.

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:47.790
The courage that it takes to do that.

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:50.000
And

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:53.870
I just got so much value out
of that because who shares

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:57.720
that kind of stuff in

00:24:57.720 --> 00:24:59.960
a live, in person?

00:24:59.960 --> 00:25:02.030
So I'm not trying to
compare me saying, oh,

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:05.960
I was feeling a little bit
bad about my career to that.

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:08.840
But I was thinking

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:11.300
this energy

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:13.490
is so--

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:15.530
like, it was so meaningful.

00:25:15.530 --> 00:25:20.480
It was such a beautiful
moment, and there was just no

00:25:20.480 --> 00:25:23.010
pure head insight
that could compare.

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:27.230
And

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:28.400
it made me feel close to them,

00:25:28.400 --> 00:25:31.220
that they were sharing something
that was so close to their--

00:25:33.290 --> 00:25:33.650
I don't know--

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:35.900
so vulnerable.

00:25:35.900 --> 00:25:39.440
So yeah, I brought it up
with my speaking coach,

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:41.550
and she was a little bit like,

00:25:41.550 --> 00:25:44.610
wait, why are you going to
do this? And I was like,

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:46.470
I don't know. I just
really love this community.

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:49.610
And I feel like whenever

00:25:49.610 --> 00:25:50.510
we--

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:52.640
the best parts of this
community are not--

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:56.570
it's not about the
technical exchange. It's--

00:25:56.570 --> 00:25:58.910
when we do have those opportunities
to get real with each other,

00:25:58.910 --> 00:26:02.700
like when people talk about
imposter syndrome from the stage,

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:06.440
those are some of the most
powerful moments. And I felt like

00:26:06.440 --> 00:26:09.710
what if I did that for
a keynote? Because that story is true.

00:26:09.710 --> 00:26:11.480
That

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:12.920
was not something I
had to conjure up.

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:14.920
I think about that
moment all the time.

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:21.170
Oh, and actually, I have
to give credit to Jesse Mostipak.

00:26:21.170 --> 00:26:23.210
I told that story to her.

00:26:23.210 --> 00:26:29.300
And she was like, get out
of town. As soon as you said

00:26:29.300 --> 00:26:32.460
that was supposed to be my last
day at RStudio, I was like,

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:35.870
tell me the rest of this
story. So she really--

00:26:35.870 --> 00:26:37.100
I have to give her, I think,

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:40.190
most of the credit for recognizing
that story was one of those--

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:42.190
it could be one
of those moments.

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:47.730
- Well, thank you, Joe.

00:26:47.730 --> 00:26:50.250
It feels like there's not
really a natural transition for

00:26:50.250 --> 00:26:53.430
me to go from this
emotional conversation to jumping back

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:56.790
into technical, but we're
just going to do it. So

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:59.580
thank you, Joe.

00:26:59.580 --> 00:27:04.370
I see that there are few
questions earlier in the chat I

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:06.060
want to get to. But Dan,

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:09.150
I see you put a question in
there about how you're starting

00:27:09.150 --> 00:27:11.700
to develop in Shiny,
coming from Python.

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:13.700
Do you want to jump
in here with that?

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:18.270
DAN: Yeah, sure,
Rachel. Hi, Joe.

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:19.800
I am

00:27:19.800 --> 00:27:21.150
more of a Python developer,

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:23.250
although I started
years and years ago,

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:26.490
many blue moons ago with and
really fell in love with it,

00:27:26.490 --> 00:27:30.090
and coming back to it a
little bit and trying to develop with

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:35.190
Shiny. One frustration I
have is just around debugging

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:40.680
and when you've got all
these panes and windows

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:43.820
and UI widgets.

00:27:43.820 --> 00:27:46.280
Can you share your
thoughts around--

00:27:46.280 --> 00:27:49.250
or best practices,
tips, whatever,

00:27:49.250 --> 00:27:54.620
on the best way to go through
the debugging process?

00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:58.730
- Yeah, absolutely. And
can you just clarify--

00:27:58.730 --> 00:28:01.820
when you say, debugging, you
mean in a more general sense,

00:28:01.820 --> 00:28:05.340
not just using an
interactive debugger?

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:10.020
DAN: Yeah, correct. Like, I do
everything in RStudio at the moment.

00:28:10.020 --> 00:28:12.900
And I run the app.
All of a sudden,

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:15.300
it just opens up
and then closes.

00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:16.770
Or I run something--

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:19.590
it spools an error, and

00:28:19.590 --> 00:28:22.560
it's not always obvious
where the error actually is.

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:26.280
It could be in the output side.
It could be on the server side.

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:31.140
And just maybe the pattern for
debugging in R Shiny's just so

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:32.970
different from
what I'm used to--

00:28:32.970 --> 00:28:34.020
that could be it, too.

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:35.710
- Yeah, it absolutely is well,

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:37.200
especially if your
work on Python--

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:38.920
was it around

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:41.250
notebooks and scripts?

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:44.180
DAN: Yeah, you got
it. Yeah, Jupyter Notebooks, scripts,

00:28:44.180 --> 00:28:44.960
that kind of thing.

00:28:44.960 --> 00:28:47.130
- Yeah, absolutely.
So I think--

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:51.470
for those of you who do
not use Shiny especially,

00:28:51.470 --> 00:28:53.720
when you were doing

00:28:53.720 --> 00:28:57.390
normal analysis with a notebook
or a script on your desktop,

00:28:57.390 --> 00:29:00.200
your code generally executes
from beginning to end, right?

00:29:00.200 --> 00:29:01.550
Like, you might
have some functions,

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:04.040
but those functions are
called when you call them.

00:29:04.040 --> 00:29:07.280
And you have loops, but
they loop when you loop.

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:11.120
It's very transparent where

00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:13.700
the interpreter is
at any given moment.

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:16.430
With Shiny and

00:29:16.430 --> 00:29:20.180
almost every other form
of interactive framework,

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:22.070
you provide

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:23.610
code to the framework,

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:26.550
and the framework
decides when it executes.

00:29:26.550 --> 00:29:29.780
So it makes it much

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:33.260
less intuitive to debug because--

00:29:33.260 --> 00:29:35.990
like, OK, this code is
executing. Why is it executing?

00:29:35.990 --> 00:29:37.990
How did it get here?

00:29:39.530 --> 00:29:41.930
It's much less transparent

00:29:41.930 --> 00:29:44.810
when code is executing,
why it's executing.

00:29:44.810 --> 00:29:48.770
So there's a couple of things
that I'll tell you from

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:51.650
a debugging perspective. Number one,
I think that Hadley's book

00:29:51.650 --> 00:29:53.720
Mastering Shiny

00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:55.140
has a chapter on this.

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:57.740
And we definitely have at least
a couple of video resources

00:29:57.740 --> 00:29:59.670
where we've given
talks about this.

00:29:59.670 --> 00:30:03.298
So I'd highly recommend
those. But I have a couple of

00:30:06.230 --> 00:30:09.710
principles that I always
go back to is as well.

00:30:09.710 --> 00:30:10.670
Number one--

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:12.170
and maybe

00:30:12.170 --> 00:30:15.890
this less applies to you
if you're coming from Python, but

00:30:15.890 --> 00:30:19.460
stack traces are
very important.

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:21.560
Often when you get
an error in Shiny,

00:30:21.560 --> 00:30:26.510
you get a big bunch of
lines spit out at the console that

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:30.320
say, from this file,
this function name--

00:30:30.320 --> 00:30:31.970
from this file, this
function name, this file,

00:30:31.970 --> 00:30:35.420
this function name. And they
often are multiple line.

00:30:35.420 --> 00:30:38.300
And often people find
that scary because

00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:40.400
a lot of the function names
that are in there are function

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:42.740
names that they might not
recognize because it's not code

00:30:42.740 --> 00:30:45.530
that they wrote that's
executing it's code that's

00:30:45.530 --> 00:30:47.900
inside of a package that's
inside of a package that's

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:50.330
calling base R.

00:30:50.330 --> 00:30:51.140
DAN: Exactly, yeah.

00:30:51.140 --> 00:30:55.250
- Yeah, so it's very important

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:58.790
to not be afraid of
that stack trace.

00:30:58.790 --> 00:31:02.030
It is very important to take
the time to learn how to read

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:04.400
those and understand
exactly what's going on.

00:31:04.400 --> 00:31:07.220
And what the stack trace is
telling you is that at the

00:31:07.220 --> 00:31:09.530
moment that this
error occurred,

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:13.490
what were the functions, in
order, that had been called?

00:31:13.490 --> 00:31:14.960
So if you think about--

00:31:14.960 --> 00:31:18.200
at any given moment, when
code is executing for R--

00:31:18.200 --> 00:31:20.330
that code is probably
in a function,

00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:22.640
and that function was
called by another function.

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:25.350
And that call was called
by another function, and that call--

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:27.710
and so on and so forth, and
it could be 20 levels deep.

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:32.470
That is called your call stack, and

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:34.150
when an error occurs,

00:31:34.150 --> 00:31:37.060
one of the most useful things
to know is what was the call

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:39.730
stack. And when that's
printed to the console,

00:31:39.730 --> 00:31:41.320
now it's called a stack trace.

00:31:41.320 --> 00:31:43.210
So you can take
that stack trace,

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:46.510
and you can read up it and
just ignore the lines that you don't

00:31:46.510 --> 00:31:49.030
recognize and find the line
that you do recognize that

00:31:49.030 --> 00:31:51.520
says, this was
the line of code.

00:31:51.520 --> 00:31:56.410
In your app.R file, it was line
49. That was the thing that was

00:31:56.410 --> 00:32:00.340
executing when this error
happened. And I think that--

00:32:00.340 --> 00:32:03.880
for the easy cases where,
let's say, I don't know--

00:32:03.880 --> 00:32:09.010
you used the wrong function
name or you indexed

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:12.670
into a data frame
using an invalid

00:32:12.670 --> 00:32:15.610
variable or
something like that--

00:32:15.610 --> 00:32:17.260
just understanding
the stack trace--

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:18.790
that

00:32:18.790 --> 00:32:22.320
will be enough for you to
fix the problem. So that's one.

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:26.630
The second thing you can
do that's super useful is--

00:32:26.630 --> 00:32:28.220
especially in Shiny--

00:32:28.220 --> 00:32:31.550
never forget that there is
a JavaScript console, also,

00:32:31.550 --> 00:32:34.970
that might be printing
errors. And this is--

00:32:34.970 --> 00:32:36.180
I feel really bad about this.

00:32:36.180 --> 00:32:39.200
We should do things in
Shiny to surface this more prominently.

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:42.320
But when you're in your browser
looking at your Shiny app,

00:32:42.320 --> 00:32:45.830
if things are not behaving the
way you expect and you don't

00:32:45.830 --> 00:32:48.710
see any errors
in the R console,

00:32:48.710 --> 00:32:52.370
you can show the JavaScript
console in your browser.

00:32:52.370 --> 00:32:55.310
And if you see a
bunch of errors there,

00:32:55.310 --> 00:32:58.730
that's a big clue
for you as well.

00:32:58.730 --> 00:32:59.150
Often,

00:32:59.150 --> 00:33:01.890
that means there's some kind of
bug in a component that you're using,

00:33:01.890 --> 00:33:05.600
whether it's in Shiny or some
kind of third party components. But

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:06.620
if

00:33:06.620 --> 00:33:08.570
you're raising a GitHub
issue or whatever,

00:33:08.570 --> 00:33:11.090
that's some of the most
useful information you can provide.

00:33:11.090 --> 00:33:12.080
The third--

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:13.700
and I'll move on after this--

00:33:13.700 --> 00:33:15.320
is to--

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:18.320
so Shiny does demand that you
write your code in a certain

00:33:18.320 --> 00:33:21.800
way. We have reactive outputs
and reactive expressions,

00:33:21.800 --> 00:33:25.280
and you have to put your code
in a certain place to get it to

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:27.330
execute at the right time.

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:30.060
That doesn't mean that your

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:31.770
data science logic--

00:33:31.770 --> 00:33:33.960
that your data analysis,
your data manipulation,

00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:35.070
and your visualization--

00:33:35.070 --> 00:33:37.020
that logic doesn't
have to live there.

00:33:37.020 --> 00:33:39.810
You can write functions
that live off to the side.

00:33:39.810 --> 00:33:41.550
They can even live in
a separate function.

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:43.770
They can even live in
a package, if you want.

00:33:43.770 --> 00:33:50.400
So you can write functions that
are not Shiny. They're just R functions

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:51.540
that

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:55.140
perform the tasks that you
want, and you call them from

00:33:55.140 --> 00:33:57.700
Shiny. You call them
from your Shiny outputs.

00:33:57.700 --> 00:33:59.670
And the advantage of
doing it this way is that,

00:33:59.670 --> 00:34:00.760
if something goes wrong,

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:03.930
you can test these pieces
in isolation from the console.

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:07.140
So you can make sure that
each of these functions that you

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:09.270
create, that do your
data manipulation,

00:34:09.270 --> 00:34:10.710
that do your data
visualization--

00:34:10.710 --> 00:34:13.060
you can ensure that they
are working correctly.

00:34:13.060 --> 00:34:16.290
And then your Shiny
app collapses to just--

00:34:16.290 --> 00:34:18.360
a very small number
of lines of code,

00:34:18.360 --> 00:34:21.449
and then it's much easier
to reason about when things are

00:34:21.449 --> 00:34:24.070
being called and why.

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:25.190
DAN: Yeah, that's helpful.

00:34:25.190 --> 00:34:29.739
I've been using the Breakpoints
and Browser and things like that

00:34:29.739 --> 00:34:31.630
a little bit, but it's still--

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:33.679
just compared to
what I'm used to--

00:34:33.679 --> 00:34:35.139
a different mental model.

00:34:35.139 --> 00:34:38.260
But I think what you laid
out is helpful, so thank you.

00:34:38.260 --> 00:34:40.659
- Yeah, the

00:34:40.659 --> 00:34:44.125
Browser and Breakpoints are super
helpful when you need them. But

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:50.020
like, on my team I'm
constantly using the phrase,

00:34:50.020 --> 00:34:51.679
can you reason
about it? I mean,

00:34:51.679 --> 00:34:53.290
they must be so sick
of me saying that,

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:56.139
but when you write code, it
is not enough that it works.

00:34:56.139 --> 00:34:57.400
If it's important
for it to be right,

00:34:57.400 --> 00:34:58.550
it's not enough that it works.

00:34:58.550 --> 00:35:00.010
You have to be able
to reason about it.

00:35:00.010 --> 00:35:03.310
And I find Browser is the
most helpful when you have lost the

00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:05.350
ability to reason
about your code.

00:35:05.350 --> 00:35:09.280
So definitely use it but
also think to yourself, like,

00:35:09.280 --> 00:35:11.590
if this was really the only way
for me to figure out what was

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:15.640
going on, maybe it's time to
refactor my code a little bit, also.

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:17.640
Oh, great.

00:35:18.580 --> 00:35:21.550
Libby Heeren said, I loved
Tan Ho's little blurb.

00:35:21.550 --> 00:35:23.290
Functions contain LogicModules.

00:35:23.290 --> 00:35:27.390
Call those functions app.R
calls and modules. That's great.

00:35:27.390 --> 00:35:27.540
- Yeah,

00:35:27.540 --> 00:35:32.820
there's a lot of great links and
resources being shared in the chat, too.

00:35:32.820 --> 00:35:36.070
Yeah, just to remind everybody
you can save the chat, too.

00:35:36.070 --> 00:35:38.550
So those little buttons
below that say More--

00:35:38.550 --> 00:35:41.900
you could save those
