00:35:41.900 --> 00:35:43.830
Joe, so another question--

00:35:43.830 --> 00:35:46.790
oh, Sam, I see you had a
question in the chat earlier.

00:35:46.790 --> 00:35:48.790
Do you want to jump in here?

00:35:50.010 --> 00:35:51.750
SAM: Yeah, sure.
Can you hear me?

00:35:51.750 --> 00:35:53.700
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Yeah.

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:54.420
- OK,

00:35:54.420 --> 00:35:56.490
so I just had a question
regarding the various

00:35:56.490 --> 00:35:59.430
development frameworks and what
your thoughts are [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:59.430 --> 00:36:01.530
the development frameworks
like 'golem,' for RInno,

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:04.410
from production-grade
development.

00:36:04.410 --> 00:36:08.190
Has Posit considered releasing their
own framework once upon a time

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:09.000
before the [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:11.370
release?

00:36:11.370 --> 00:36:12.390
- Yeah, the question is--

00:36:12.390 --> 00:36:15.210
if you guys are not familiar,

00:36:15.210 --> 00:36:20.550
there are a couple of packages
that have been created

00:36:20.550 --> 00:36:27.150
to provide more opinionated structure
around how Shiny apps are written.

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:31.470
So 'golem' and RInno
are probably the two--

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:33.470
by far, the two most popular.

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:37.640
I have to say--

00:36:37.640 --> 00:36:40.250
I'm a little embarrassed. Like,
this is a very important topic,

00:36:40.250 --> 00:36:44.060
and I know people have
really strong opinions about this.

00:36:44.060 --> 00:36:48.560
And in particular, I
think 'golem' has huge

00:36:48.560 --> 00:36:51.920
proponents among people
that I know and respect.

00:36:51.920 --> 00:36:53.090
I

00:36:53.090 --> 00:36:55.880
personally have not
spent that much time

00:36:55.880 --> 00:36:58.640
with these
frameworks. I think--

00:36:58.640 --> 00:37:01.850
just the tradition that I
come from in my background in

00:37:01.850 --> 00:37:03.850
software engineering--

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:11.870
I feel like a lot of people--

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:14.390
sorry, this is not the
only reason people use these frameworks.

00:37:14.390 --> 00:37:17.000
But I do think that part of--

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:19.280
one thing that I've noticed
that people really like in a

00:37:19.280 --> 00:37:21.410
lot of parts of
data science is--

00:37:21.410 --> 00:37:24.710
especially if you didn't set
out to become a programmer but

00:37:24.710 --> 00:37:26.480
accidentally became
a programmer,

00:37:26.480 --> 00:37:28.940
I think a lot of
people feel like,

00:37:28.940 --> 00:37:31.070
if they're doing what
it says in the tutorial,

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:33.800
if they're doing what it says
in an article that Hadley

00:37:33.800 --> 00:37:36.080
wrote, then they're
doing it right.

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:39.260
And if they're doing something
that they invented or a pattern

00:37:39.260 --> 00:37:42.470
that they just came
up with, then, ah,

00:37:42.470 --> 00:37:45.420
that's a hack, or that's
a workaround or whatever.

00:37:45.420 --> 00:37:47.780
So I've had a lot of
people ask me things

00:37:47.780 --> 00:37:52.350
on the Shiny issues or
Shiny forums and say,

00:37:52.350 --> 00:37:55.160
hey, how do I
solve this problem?

00:37:55.160 --> 00:37:57.650
Or it would be great if
you had this feature.

00:37:57.650 --> 00:38:00.290
I solved it this way, but it
would be nice to have it for

00:38:00.290 --> 00:38:02.320
real. And it's like--

00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:04.420
the way you did it is perfect.

00:38:04.420 --> 00:38:08.000
What about this solution
does not satisfy you?

00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:10.060
And they're like, yeah,
well, but I did it,

00:38:10.060 --> 00:38:13.660
so I would really like
a grown up to do it.

00:38:13.660 --> 00:38:15.280
And

00:38:15.280 --> 00:38:17.050
I think, in the beginning,

00:38:17.050 --> 00:38:19.900
it felt like a lot of
people were looking for a lot more

00:38:19.900 --> 00:38:25.660
guidance for how to structure
their Shiny apps, and 'golem' provided

00:38:25.660 --> 00:38:26.530
a set of opinions--

00:38:26.530 --> 00:38:28.840
that people could look to

00:38:28.840 --> 00:38:31.540
Vincent and Colin and
say, those people are clearly

00:38:31.540 --> 00:38:34.480
experts. If I use 'golem,' then

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:36.460
I'm allowed to do that,

00:38:36.460 --> 00:38:40.030
whereas I had much more
of an attitude of--

00:38:40.030 --> 00:38:40.570
it's R,

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:44.150
so you should feel free to
package these things however you want,

00:38:44.150 --> 00:38:48.400
and you don't need anybody to
tell you that that's OK or not.

00:38:48.400 --> 00:38:51.460
I do totally reserve
the right to judge you,

00:38:51.460 --> 00:38:53.690
but you should do it anyway.

00:38:53.690 --> 00:38:55.990
So

00:38:55.990 --> 00:38:59.350
I think, over the
years, though, it has--

00:38:59.350 --> 00:39:02.380
more and more
people have told me

00:39:02.380 --> 00:39:03.470
it is not just about that.

00:39:03.470 --> 00:39:05.950
It's not just a set of opinions
that are nice to have--

00:39:05.950 --> 00:39:08.140
that they actually

00:39:08.140 --> 00:39:11.110
do try to provide a

00:39:11.110 --> 00:39:15.010
lower friction environment for you
to do development and have

00:39:15.010 --> 00:39:20.140
been very thoughtful about how you
run and reload your app. And

00:39:20.140 --> 00:39:22.930
I totally believe
that it has value.

00:39:22.930 --> 00:39:23.470
I have to admit,

00:39:23.470 --> 00:39:26.200
I have underinvested in
spending time with those.

00:39:26.200 --> 00:39:29.105
But I certainly--

00:39:29.105 --> 00:39:33.940
certainly, I expect those
things to work well given how

00:39:33.940 --> 00:39:35.940
much people use them.

00:39:38.540 --> 00:39:42.980
- To go back to the
question right before this,

00:39:42.980 --> 00:39:44.720
there was a follow-up
question about--

00:39:44.720 --> 00:39:47.480
could you say a little bit
more about what you mean by,

00:39:47.480 --> 00:39:49.750
can you reason about it?

00:39:49.750 --> 00:39:52.980
I think it might be
helpful for us all, yeah.

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:54.980
- My favorite topic.

00:39:56.680 --> 00:39:59.390
Yeah, so

00:39:59.390 --> 00:40:01.390
I think--

00:40:02.550 --> 00:40:06.220
just to reiterate--

00:40:06.220 --> 00:40:10.890
when you write software or
do an analysis or whatever,

00:40:10.890 --> 00:40:11.880
there's a level of--

00:40:11.880 --> 00:40:13.470
I got it to work.

00:40:13.470 --> 00:40:15.480
And then there's the level of--

00:40:15.480 --> 00:40:17.590
it works, and I can
reason about it.

00:40:17.590 --> 00:40:20.204
And what that
means is software--

00:40:22.530 --> 00:40:27.540
complex pieces of software
are among the most complicated

00:40:27.540 --> 00:40:30.300
things that humankind
has ever devised.

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:35.340
What other human-made
constructs can have

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:39.080
hundreds of millions of pieces
and yet they're expected to all

00:40:39.080 --> 00:40:43.650
fit and work together so
precisely that, if one token is

00:40:43.650 --> 00:40:46.110
off, rockets

00:40:46.110 --> 00:40:48.020
explode and

00:40:48.020 --> 00:40:50.020
you get the wrong answers?

00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:52.230
And

00:40:52.230 --> 00:40:55.080
I don't know what
the limit is, but

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:56.250
even the smartest humans--

00:40:56.250 --> 00:41:00.010
there is some small number of
variables you can hold in your head.

00:41:00.010 --> 00:41:02.790
There's a small number of
operations that you can hold in

00:41:02.790 --> 00:41:05.050
your head at any one time.

00:41:05.050 --> 00:41:06.460
So

00:41:06.460 --> 00:41:09.280
when we work on software
that's non-trivial,

00:41:09.280 --> 00:41:12.090
when we work on software that,

00:41:12.090 --> 00:41:15.070
in its totality, is more
than the human mind--

00:41:15.070 --> 00:41:19.570
more than any one human mind
can hold at any one given time,

00:41:19.570 --> 00:41:23.260
the main challenge in
software engineering is about,

00:41:23.260 --> 00:41:26.800
how do you take all this
complexity and break it down

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:31.930
into smaller pieces, each of
which you can reason about,

00:41:31.930 --> 00:41:33.490
each of which you can
hold in your head,

00:41:33.490 --> 00:41:36.550
each of which you can
look at and be like,

00:41:36.550 --> 00:41:40.470
yeah, I can fully--

00:41:40.470 --> 00:41:47.130
I can fully ingest this entire
function definition. I can read it,

00:41:47.130 --> 00:41:49.750
line by line, and
prove to myself,

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:53.160
this is definitely correct if
the functions that it's calling

00:41:53.160 --> 00:41:56.020
don't have bugs and if it's
called in the right way.

00:41:56.020 --> 00:41:57.540
So with those caveats,

00:41:57.540 --> 00:42:00.510
if the things that this thing
is calling are correct and I'm

00:42:00.510 --> 00:42:01.530
called correctly,

00:42:01.530 --> 00:42:05.760
then the result will be correct
because the logic here is correct.

00:42:05.760 --> 00:42:06.990
So

00:42:06.990 --> 00:42:11.250
software engineering, at all
but the most beginner level,

00:42:11.250 --> 00:42:15.810
is a lot about this. How
do you break up inherently

00:42:15.810 --> 00:42:19.980
complicated things that we're trying
to do into small pieces

00:42:19.980 --> 00:42:23.850
that are individually
easy to reason about ?

00:42:23.850 --> 00:42:25.720
And that's half the
battle right there.

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:26.970
The other half
of the battle is,

00:42:26.970 --> 00:42:34.650
how do we combine them in ways
that can be reliable and also easy to

00:42:34.650 --> 00:42:35.100
reason about?

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:37.170
So

00:42:37.170 --> 00:42:38.520
it's these two pieces--

00:42:38.520 --> 00:42:40.440
small pieces

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:41.850
reliably composed--

00:42:41.850 --> 00:42:45.030
if you can achieve that,
that's what I'm talking about.

00:42:45.030 --> 00:42:48.130
That's software that
you can reason about.

00:42:48.130 --> 00:42:50.130
So

00:42:50.930 --> 00:42:58.040
this has implications for data science as
well. When you have a data science,

00:42:58.040 --> 00:43:00.860
you're doing some kind
of analysis on some data,

00:43:00.860 --> 00:43:04.530
and it starts out as, oh,
I'm just doing these simple things.

00:43:04.530 --> 00:43:06.230
I'm doing this manipulation,

00:43:06.230 --> 00:43:08.300
and then I'm doing
this visualization.

00:43:08.300 --> 00:43:10.310
But then as you get
deeper and deeper into it,

00:43:10.310 --> 00:43:12.050
it grows and grows
and grows and grows.

00:43:12.050 --> 00:43:14.840
And you're at the point
where you're at the end,

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:18.140
and you don't remember
where these variables came from.

00:43:18.140 --> 00:43:19.610
You don't remember

00:43:19.610 --> 00:43:21.740
what's the difference between this
data frame and this data

00:43:21.740 --> 00:43:25.280
frame. And you go back and

00:43:25.280 --> 00:43:29.780
hopefully start breaking it
into functions or somehow

00:43:29.780 --> 00:43:33.740
dividing it into smaller pieces
that each focus on a thing.

00:43:33.740 --> 00:43:37.130
And then you join those
pieces together in your overall script.

00:43:37.130 --> 00:43:39.020
So that is--

00:43:39.020 --> 00:43:44.660
that's this principle of
small pieces individually

00:43:44.660 --> 00:43:47.000
able to be reasoned about. And

00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:48.920
when you think about other
rules that you might have heard

00:43:48.920 --> 00:43:53.300
about software
engineering, we know,

00:43:53.300 --> 00:43:55.880
when you're writing functions,
using global variables is bad.

00:43:55.880 --> 00:43:58.280
That's another one of these
things where that hurts your

00:43:58.280 --> 00:44:00.740
ability to hold the entire
function in your head and to

00:44:00.740 --> 00:44:04.370
prove that it'll work correctly
because who knows who's setting

00:44:04.370 --> 00:44:06.660
that global variable
to what value?

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:10.700
So you can't prove to
yourself this function is definitely correct.

00:44:12.940 --> 00:44:14.940
So

00:44:15.580 --> 00:44:17.830
I think--

00:44:17.830 --> 00:44:21.760
if there's anything that you're working
on that needs to be correct--

00:44:21.760 --> 00:44:25.010
if you're just zipping off some
analysis and it really doesn't matter.

00:44:25.010 --> 00:44:26.320
You just a pretty picture
or something like that or

00:44:26.320 --> 00:44:28.180
whatever. I don't know
what that situation is.

00:44:28.180 --> 00:44:30.250
But if you don't care if
the answer is right, sure.

00:44:30.250 --> 00:44:32.230
But if you do care that
the answer is right,

00:44:32.230 --> 00:44:36.130
I try never to stop when I have

00:44:36.130 --> 00:44:38.200
an answer but I can't
reason about the code.

00:44:38.200 --> 00:44:41.560
I always try to go back and
do the refactoring that's

00:44:41.560 --> 00:44:45.430
necessary just so I can prove to
myself that the answer is right. Oh,

00:44:45.430 --> 00:44:48.700
and the other big benefit to
this is that those individual pieces--

00:44:48.700 --> 00:44:50.770
if it does turn out that
there's a mistakes somewhere,

00:44:50.770 --> 00:44:53.230
you can individually
debug, test,

00:44:53.230 --> 00:44:57.220
unit test those
individual pieces.

00:44:57.220 --> 00:44:58.960
And when there are problems,

00:44:58.960 --> 00:45:01.330
you'll much more easily
be able to find them.

00:45:04.350 --> 00:45:07.500
- Great. Thank you. So I see

00:45:07.500 --> 00:45:11.010
an anonymous question that
touches on what you've asked as

00:45:11.010 --> 00:45:13.920
well, Alan, so I'm going to
ask you to jump in here, too,

00:45:13.920 --> 00:45:15.920
first, with your question.

00:45:17.570 --> 00:45:19.660
- Yeah, sure, thanks.

00:45:19.660 --> 00:45:22.090
Hi, Joe, I'm really
curious about--

00:45:22.090 --> 00:45:25.300
you mentioned earlier that you and the
team had to think a lot about--

00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:26.440
and you were nervous about--

00:45:26.440 --> 00:45:28.270
how do we learn Python?

00:45:28.270 --> 00:45:31.990
How do we learn what we need
to know here to do Shiny for Python?

00:45:31.990 --> 00:45:32.920
I'm really curious--

00:45:32.920 --> 00:45:34.780
now that it's--

00:45:34.780 --> 00:45:40.030
you're trying to gain momentum with
it, trying to establish it, et cetera,

00:45:40.030 --> 00:45:42.670
have you had to come up
with a different way to approach the

00:45:42.670 --> 00:45:47.260
Python community than maybe you would have
taken for granted in the R community?

00:45:47.260 --> 00:45:51.430
We know that community has changed
so much over 15, 20 years.

00:45:51.430 --> 00:45:53.770
What's it like to come into
the Python community now,

00:45:53.770 --> 00:45:58.720
and how did that feel? What
was successful? Was it hard?

00:45:58.720 --> 00:46:00.640
Curious how you come
into that space.

00:46:00.640 --> 00:46:07.890
- It is 1,000% different. It
is completely and utterly different. And

00:46:07.890 --> 00:46:10.380
it's

00:46:10.380 --> 00:46:12.540
hard to enumerate how
many differences that there are

00:46:12.540 --> 00:46:16.970
because there are so many. But
the biggest, by far, is that--

00:46:16.970 --> 00:46:20.280
I mean, Python is
just such a noisy

00:46:20.280 --> 00:46:22.280
space. Well,

00:46:23.400 --> 00:46:28.590
that's a really uncharitable word. It's
a very vibrant and active space.

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:37.140
I think, in the early days
of RStudio, the company,

00:46:37.140 --> 00:46:38.940
there

00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:43.410
was a lot of
interest in activity around R,

00:46:43.410 --> 00:46:44.700
but

00:46:44.700 --> 00:46:47.100
it was not like
Python is today.

00:46:47.100 --> 00:46:49.375
Python--

00:46:49.375 --> 00:46:50.260
you

00:46:50.260 --> 00:46:55.960
can't think of any niche and not
find 15 packages that do that thing,

00:46:55.960 --> 00:46:59.690
and there are two more
venture-backed ones coming out next week.

00:46:59.690 --> 00:47:00.490
It's just--

00:47:00.490 --> 00:47:06.760
the level of activity is
on such a different level. And

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:10.030
it's quite--

00:47:10.030 --> 00:47:13.930
it was quite a wake-up
call to come into that,

00:47:13.930 --> 00:47:19.330
going to conferences and things like
that, and realizing, OK, not only

00:47:19.330 --> 00:47:23.870
are we not a well-known
entity in the Python world,

00:47:23.870 --> 00:47:28.950
especially after we
rebranded the company. But

00:47:28.950 --> 00:47:31.890
like the amount that you would
have to scream to get over this

00:47:31.890 --> 00:47:36.780
noise is just incredible.

00:47:36.780 --> 00:47:41.840
So that was one big takeaway,
is that we really don't have

00:47:41.840 --> 00:47:44.870
the luxury of being heard

00:47:44.870 --> 00:47:46.820
or taking for granted that
we're going to be heard with

00:47:46.820 --> 00:47:48.560
any particular
message that we have.

00:47:48.560 --> 00:47:53.150
And that makes it so much more
important that when we tell our story,

00:47:53.150 --> 00:47:56.930
that we know exactly who we're
telling it to and that we know

00:47:56.930 --> 00:47:58.790
exactly what's going to
resonate with them because I

00:47:58.790 --> 00:48:01.160
think, in the R community,

00:48:01.160 --> 00:48:02.610
when Shiny came out,

00:48:02.610 --> 00:48:05.280
people were willing to
spend the time to find out,

00:48:05.280 --> 00:48:06.530
does this really
make sense for me?

00:48:06.530 --> 00:48:08.600
And they were willing
to do some work.

00:48:08.600 --> 00:48:10.160
And

00:48:10.160 --> 00:48:12.160
for Python,

00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:17.920
there's 15 other options, so why would you--

00:48:17.920 --> 00:48:20.910
and there are so many very--

00:48:20.910 --> 00:48:21.450
I think,

00:48:21.450 --> 00:48:24.810
because it's such a big
community and there's so much--

00:48:24.810 --> 00:48:26.910
at least it's perceived that
there's so much money to be

00:48:26.910 --> 00:48:30.000
made, being content
creators for this community,

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:33.390
or whatever other reasons people
have for doing content creation--

00:48:33.390 --> 00:48:36.630
I've heard that there is a
content treadmill that people

00:48:36.630 --> 00:48:39.420
get on that adds to the
noise, where it's like,

00:48:39.420 --> 00:48:41.640
in order for the algorithm
to take me seriously,

00:48:41.640 --> 00:48:44.160
I have to create a new

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:45.180
Python

00:48:45.180 --> 00:48:47.550
package shoot-out every--

00:48:47.550 --> 00:48:51.660
twice a week. And you can
only do so much research

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:53.760
in three days, so you
end up with just--

00:48:53.760 --> 00:48:57.900
this incredible volume
of very shallow noise.

00:48:57.900 --> 00:49:01.830
And that's just not something I
ever remember being a big part

00:49:01.830 --> 00:49:04.200
of the community.

00:49:04.200 --> 00:49:06.830
And I know it's--

00:49:06.830 --> 00:49:09.770
for Python users, it's both
a blessing and a curse.

00:49:09.770 --> 00:49:13.430
It's awesome to know that
whatever it is that you want to

00:49:13.430 --> 00:49:15.770
solve, somebody is out there

00:49:15.770 --> 00:49:19.550
with either a free or
paid solution for you.

00:49:19.550 --> 00:49:23.660
But now the work is figuring
out which makes sense,

00:49:23.660 --> 00:49:28.310
and a lot of the analysis out
there is not going to be too helpful.

00:49:28.310 --> 00:49:29.510
I will say, though,

00:49:29.510 --> 00:49:33.800
that in terms of the people
that I've met in the community,

00:49:33.800 --> 00:49:36.500
there are two really
big takeaways that I have--

00:49:36.500 --> 00:49:37.550
number one--

00:49:37.550 --> 00:49:41.270
that almost everybody
that I've met has been

00:49:41.270 --> 00:49:47.060
very kind and generous
to talk with us and

00:49:47.060 --> 00:49:49.790
pretty nice. And

00:49:49.790 --> 00:49:50.690
I don't know--

00:49:50.690 --> 00:49:54.260
I had heard people in
the R community say,

00:49:54.260 --> 00:49:57.260
I have been in the Python
community, and boy,

00:49:57.260 --> 00:49:59.640
it's so refreshing to
be here and whatever.

00:49:59.640 --> 00:50:00.890
And

00:50:00.890 --> 00:50:04.220
at least the particular
conferences that I've been to,

00:50:04.220 --> 00:50:06.860
and being 2022 or 2023--

00:50:06.860 --> 00:50:11.450
I don't know how it
was 10 years ago. But yeah,

00:50:11.450 --> 00:50:14.120
I really saw a lot of the
same things that I like in the R

00:50:14.120 --> 00:50:16.700
community happening in
the Python community,

00:50:16.700 --> 00:50:20.690
maybe without the slightly more tightly

00:50:20.690 --> 00:50:24.170
knit feeling that comes from

00:50:24.170 --> 00:50:27.650
being a small community
for so many years.

00:50:27.650 --> 00:50:29.920
But the other thing is that
it's such a big community that

00:50:29.920 --> 00:50:34.720
I feel like it is very,
very hard to generalize what people--

00:50:34.720 --> 00:50:38.530
what a Python data
scientist even is.

00:50:38.530 --> 00:50:42.910
It's hard to even divide
it into four or five groups.

00:50:42.910 --> 00:50:46.750
There's so much diversity.
There are people who

00:50:46.750 --> 00:50:49.830
are like so much more
commercial than the average R

00:50:49.830 --> 00:50:52.630
user is, who are so
much more focused on--

00:50:52.630 --> 00:50:54.630
this

00:50:55.500 --> 00:51:00.330
is a purely commercial interest
for them, to people who

00:51:00.330 --> 00:51:05.340
are so much more extreme
free software zealots than I have

00:51:05.340 --> 00:51:08.400
ever met. And that's
coming from someone who obviously

00:51:08.400 --> 00:51:10.330
really, really believes
in open source software.

00:51:10.330 --> 00:51:14.640
I had a couple people ask
us at PyData Seattle--

00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:17.880
so you have all this open
source stuff, but you also have

00:51:17.880 --> 00:51:19.880
these complimentary

00:51:21.940 --> 00:51:24.900
commercial offerings.

00:51:24.900 --> 00:51:27.420
To me, you don't
count as open source.

00:51:27.420 --> 00:51:31.140
It's like, what?
Or people who say,

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:34.410
because your governance model is
not open and you don't have

00:51:34.410 --> 00:51:39.360
a democratically elected board, you're
not open source. I'm like--

00:51:39.360 --> 00:51:41.670
[LAUGHS]

00:51:41.670 --> 00:51:45.210
Cool that you have that
position. Totally respect that.

00:51:45.210 --> 00:51:47.010
But to say that, open source,

00:51:47.010 --> 00:51:49.140
is the word that means
the thing that I like--

00:51:49.140 --> 00:51:49.800
I'm like, wow,

00:51:49.800 --> 00:51:52.380
this is not something that I
have ever encountered in the R

00:51:52.380 --> 00:51:54.210
world. So again,

00:51:54.210 --> 00:51:55.650
the whole spectrum

00:51:55.650 --> 00:51:58.410
has been represented at each
of the conferences I've been to.

00:51:58.410 --> 00:52:01.890
And I guess I was a little
bit surprised that it is not just

00:52:01.890 --> 00:52:04.540
such a big community but so

00:52:04.540 --> 00:52:06.540
incredibly diverse.

00:52:08.890 --> 00:52:11.260
- Awesome. Thank you. I
appreciate hearing all of that

00:52:11.260 --> 00:52:14.560
so much. Really, really
grateful for your thoughts on

00:52:14.560 --> 00:52:16.970
that. Thanks, Joe.

00:52:16.970 --> 00:52:20.360
- Thanks, Joe. I see
there was an anonymous question from--

00:52:20.360 --> 00:52:21.860
actually, towards
the beginning--

00:52:21.860 --> 00:52:24.980
I want to make sure I get
to as well. And it was,

00:52:24.980 --> 00:52:29.720
I get questions from it and
leadership about data security with Shiny apps.

00:52:29.720 --> 00:52:34.820
What training or tools do you
recommend for testing our Shiny apps--

00:52:34.820 --> 00:52:36.950
for example, like, pen test?

00:52:36.950 --> 00:52:38.510
- Yeah,

00:52:38.510 --> 00:52:40.500
that is a really good question.

00:52:40.500 --> 00:52:43.460
I used to have good answers
for this because we would receive

00:52:43.460 --> 00:52:46.820
the pen test
reports from people.

00:52:46.820 --> 00:52:48.820
And I will say

00:52:50.860 --> 00:52:52.660
two things about those

00:52:52.660 --> 00:52:54.490
pen tests. I think,

00:52:54.490 --> 00:52:58.580
one, I was surprised how well

00:52:58.580 --> 00:52:59.840
those

00:52:59.840 --> 00:53:03.110
pen test tools
worked against Shiny,

00:53:03.110 --> 00:53:05.330
in the sense that the
tools didn't break. Like,

00:53:05.330 --> 00:53:08.060
they did actually run their
tests and treat Shiny as no

00:53:08.060 --> 00:53:11.580
different than any other web
app that they would test.

00:53:11.580 --> 00:53:15.230
So that was cool to see,
and there were results that,

00:53:15.230 --> 00:53:19.070
once in a blue moon, would
actually be relevant.

00:53:19.070 --> 00:53:22.670
But on the other hand, I think,

00:53:22.670 --> 00:53:27.620
in my experience, the
false positive ratio to

00:53:27.620 --> 00:53:29.810
true security issue--

00:53:29.810 --> 00:53:31.400
that ratio is--

00:53:31.400 --> 00:53:34.220
it's really surprisingly noisy,

00:53:34.220 --> 00:53:36.050
and

00:53:36.050 --> 00:53:39.570
noisy in a scary way because

00:53:39.570 --> 00:53:42.690
they sound very, very
plausible. These reports come

00:53:42.690 --> 00:53:44.640
back, and they sound
very, very plausible.

00:53:44.640 --> 00:53:46.950
But like nine times out of 10,

00:53:46.950 --> 00:53:50.940
they're just using heuristics
and don't really understand

00:53:50.940 --> 00:53:53.760
what the parameters they are
looking at and what value they

00:53:53.760 --> 00:53:58.300
have. So I would say, if
your organization says to use them,

00:53:58.300 --> 00:54:03.270
I would expect whatever pen
test whatever web-based pen test tool

00:54:03.270 --> 00:54:05.620
that you're using to work.

00:54:05.620 --> 00:54:06.780
But

00:54:06.780 --> 00:54:09.210
if you have red flags come up,

00:54:09.210 --> 00:54:11.790
expect to have to go
through our support

00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:14.190
and talk it through with them.

00:54:14.190 --> 00:54:17.730
In terms of actually
improving your security,

00:54:17.730 --> 00:54:21.870
I think a higher value
thing is to read the--

00:54:21.870 --> 00:54:23.040
there's a very standard--

00:54:23.040 --> 00:54:25.050
I think OWASP is the acronym--

00:54:25.050 --> 00:54:26.940
O-W-A-S-P--

00:54:26.940 --> 00:54:29.460
the Open Web Application
Security Project or something

00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:33.960
like that? And they have an
ever evolving list of the top

00:54:33.960 --> 00:54:35.490
10 vulnerabilities,

00:54:35.490 --> 00:54:38.460
classes of vulnerabilities
you need to worry about

00:54:38.460 --> 00:54:40.560
when you're building
your web applications.

00:54:40.560 --> 00:54:43.950
And I found those to be

00:54:43.950 --> 00:54:45.780
consistently excellent advice.

00:54:45.780 --> 00:54:48.330
Every one of those
vulnerabilities is one you

00:54:48.330 --> 00:54:51.130
should at least be aware of.

00:54:51.130 --> 00:54:52.500
And

00:54:52.500 --> 00:54:54.330
if you're a more
experienced Shiny developer,

00:54:54.330 --> 00:54:58.350
hopefully you should be able to at least

00:54:58.350 --> 00:55:00.780
be able to connect the
dots between how they're expressing

00:55:00.780 --> 00:55:05.160
the problem and how that
might affect your Shiny app. Like,

00:55:05.160 --> 00:55:06.690
code injection is one example,

00:55:06.690 --> 00:55:08.550
where you take a
parameter from a user--

00:55:08.550 --> 00:55:11.490
and the classic one is you
just take whatever name they typed

00:55:11.490 --> 00:55:13.560
in, and you just

00:55:13.560 --> 00:55:17.010
glom that onto the end of a
SQL query string that you have

00:55:17.010 --> 00:55:19.060
and put like quotes around it.

00:55:19.060 --> 00:55:21.160
But

00:55:21.160 --> 00:55:23.230
that input is coming
from the user.

00:55:23.230 --> 00:55:24.990
They could put

00:55:24.990 --> 00:55:27.990
a single quote in their

00:55:27.990 --> 00:55:30.540
input, and that would
finish your string.

00:55:30.540 --> 00:55:35.700
And now their input is being
interpreted as SQL code.

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:37.410
There's a famous
xkcd about this.

00:55:37.410 --> 00:55:40.640
If you google for Bobby Tables

00:55:40.640 --> 00:55:44.070
you'll find it. So

00:55:44.070 --> 00:55:46.560
that's an example
of a SQL injection.

00:55:46.560 --> 00:55:48.900
That's totally something
that, if you weren't paying

00:55:48.900 --> 00:55:51.480
attention, you could
introduce into a Shiny app.

00:55:51.480 --> 00:55:56.130
You could form a SQL query
that you're about to send to

00:55:56.130 --> 00:55:59.430
Oracle or PostgreSQL and add user
input to the end so there

00:55:59.430 --> 00:56:03.330
are things that you need to do
to not do that. So for sure,

00:56:03.330 --> 00:56:07.140
run the pen test if your
organization does that. But I

00:56:07.140 --> 00:56:09.900
think, more important,
read through the OWASP

00:56:12.390 --> 00:56:14.390
list of vulnerabilities.

